<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-mixed-strategies" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Mixed Strategies and the Fundamental Theorem</title>

  <p>
    Chapter 5 begins our study of mixed strategy Nash equilibria. And the
    mathematics starts to get a bit more interesting!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-1-setup">
    <title>Section 5.1, Mixed Strategies and the Definition</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 5.1 from the beginning up to Theorem 5.1 (Nash's
      Existence Theorem).</alert> Stop right before it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Take a minute to check the book's claim that Tennis has no (pure
      strategy) Nash equilibrium, using best responses. After that, most of
      the reading is new notation for mixed strategies. We'll go through it
      below and relate it to the game of Tennis.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here's the Tennis payoff matrix with each player's probabilities written
    in the margins. This is how the book draws it in Section 5.2.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-tennis-pq">
    <title>Tennis, with mixed strategies</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <col right="minor"/>
      <col/>
      <col/>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell colspan="2">Receiver</cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>q</m></cell>
        <cell><m>{1-q}</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell/>
        <cell>backhand<nbsp/>(<m>b</m>)</cell>
        <cell>forehand<nbsp/>(<m>f</m>)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Server</cell>
        <cell><m>p</m></cell>
        <cell>backhand<nbsp/>(<m>b</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(.4, .6)</cell>
        <cell>(.8, .2)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell/>
        <cell><m>{1-p}</m></cell>
        <cell>forehand<nbsp/>(<m>f</m>)</cell>
        <cell>(.7, .3)</cell>
        <cell>(.1, .9)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    So a mixed strategy for the server looks like
    <me>
      \sigma_1 = pb + (1-p)f
    </me>
    and a mixed strategy for the receiver looks like
    <me>
      \sigma_2 = qb + (1-q)f,
    </me>
    where <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> can be any numbers from <m>0</m> to
    <m>1</m>. Each choice of <m>p</m> gives a different mixed strategy for
    the server, and each choice of <m>q</m> gives a different one for the
    receiver.
  </p>

  <p>
    Also, <m>.6b + .4f</m> isn't arithmetic; you can't add strategies!! It's
    just a compact way to denote the mixed strategy of serving to the
    backhand 60% of the time and the forehand 40% of the time.
  </p>

  <p>
    The checkpoint below will check your understanding of the terms
    <q>pure</q> and <q>active</q> strategy.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-active-pure">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider two mixed strategies for the server,
        <m>\sigma_1 = .6b + .4f</m> and <m>\tau_1 = 1b + 0f</m> (we can shorten this to
        <m>\tau_1 = b</m>). Which is true?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Both <m>b</m> and <m>f</m> are active in <m>\tau_1</m>, since
            both show up in <m>1b + 0f</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Showing up isn't enough. <m>f</m> has probability <m>0</m> in
            <m>\tau_1</m>, so it's never used and isn't active.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\tau_1</m> is a mixed strategy, so it can't be a pure strategy.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Pure strategies are special mixed strategies, the ones with only
            one active strategy. <m>\tau_1</m> is both mixed and pure.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Both <m>b</m> and <m>f</m> are active in <m>\sigma_1</m>. Only
            <m>b</m> is active in <m>\tau_1</m>, so <m>\tau_1</m> is the pure
            strategy <m>b</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A strategy is active if its probability is more than
            <m>0</m>, and a mixed strategy is pure when only one strategy is
            active. So everything from Chapter 3 is still here: pure
            strategies are just special mixed strategies.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Only <m>b</m> is active in <m>\sigma_1</m>, since she uses it more
            than half the time.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Active just means the probability is more than <m>0</m>. The
            server uses <m>f</m> 40% of the time, so <m>f</m> is active too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's break down equation (5.2). In Tennis, the server's expected
    payoff adds up all four cells of <xref ref="table-tennis-pq"/>, each
    weighted by how likely we are to land in it:
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) =
      \underbrace{pq}_{\substack{\text{prob. server goes } b\\ \text{and receiver expects } b}}
      \cdot
      \underbrace{(.4)}_{\substack{\text{server's payoff}\\ \text{in that cell}}}
      + p(1-q)(.8) + (1-p)q(.7) + (1-p)(1-q)(.1)
    </me>
    Try it out below. Click any cell to see which term of the sum it
    matches.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-tennis-expected-payoff">
    <caption>
      Each cell of the Tennis matrix gives one term of the server's expected
      payoff.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-tennis-expected-payoff"
      iframe="ch5-graph/tennis-expected-payoff.html" width="100%" aspect="7:3">
      <static>
        <p>
          The Tennis payoff matrix, with <m>p</m> and <m>1-p</m> beside the
          server's rows and <m>q</m> and <m>1-q</m> above the receiver's
          columns, sits above the equation
          <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) = pq(.4) + p(1-q)(.8) + (1-p)q(.7) +
          (1-p)(1-q)(.1)</m>. Clicking a cell highlights its matching term,
          and clicking a term highlights its cell. Each term is the server's
          probability for that row times the receiver's probability for that
          column, times the first number in the cell.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The general version says the same thing:
    <me>
      \pi_i(\sigma_1, \ldots, \sigma_n) = \sum_{\text{all } (s_1, \ldots, s_n)}
      \underbrace{p_{s_1} p_{s_2} \cdots p_{s_n}}_{\substack{\text{prob. that } s_1, \ldots, s_n\\ \text{all happen at once}}}
      \;
      \underbrace{\pi_i(s_1, \ldots, s_n)}_{\substack{\text{Player } i\text{'s payoff}\\ \text{when they do}}}
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Equation (5.6) is the same sum, just grouped by Player <m>i</m>'s
    strategies. In Tennis, group the four cells by the server's row:
    <me>
      \pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) = p \cdot \pi_1(b, \sigma_2) + (1-p) \cdot \pi_1(f, \sigma_2)
    </me>
    Here <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m> is what the server gets if she always serves
    to the backhand while the receiver keeps mixing. The general version:
    <me>
      \pi_i(\sigma) = \sum_{\text{all } s_i \in S_i}
      \underbrace{p_{s_i}}_{\substack{\text{how often}\\ i \text{ plays } s_i}}
      \;
      \underbrace{\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i})}_{\substack{\text{what } s_i \text{ earns her against}\\ \text{everyone else's mixing}}}
    </me>
    So a player's payoff from a mixed strategy is a weighted average of what
    her pure strategies earn. This gives the expected payoff of a mixed
    strategy!!
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, notice the definition of a Nash equilibrium is the same as the
    Chapter 3 definition; we just allow mixed strategies now. A mixed strategy
    profile <m>\sigma^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium if for each player <m>i</m>,
    <me>
      \underbrace{\pi_i(\sigma^*)}_{\substack{i\text{'s payoff when everyone}\\ \text{plays their equilibrium strategy}}}
      \;\geq\;
      \underbrace{\pi_i(\sigma_i, \sigma^*_{-i})}_{\substack{i\text{'s payoff if she alone}\\ \text{switches to another mixed strategy}}}
      \quad \text{for every mixed strategy } \sigma_i.
    </me>
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's test a specific profile in Tennis: <m>\sigma_1 = .6b + .4f</m> and
    <m>\sigma_2 = .7b + .3f</m>. Section 5.2 will show where these numbers come
    from.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-tennis-pi1-b">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The receiver uses <m>\sigma_2 = .7b + .3f</m>. Using
        <xref ref="table-tennis-pq"/>, which computation gives
        <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m>, the server's expected payoff if she always
        serves to the backhand?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.7(.6) + .3(.2) = .48</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            You used the second number in each cell, which is the receiver's
            payoff. <m>\pi_1</m> is the server's payoff, so use the first
            numbers.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>.7(.4) + .3(.8) = .52</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Read across the server's <m>b</m> row using her payoffs
            (the first numbers), weighted by the receiver's probabilities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.6(.4) + .4(.8) = .56</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            You weighted by <m>.6</m> and <m>.4</m>, which are the server's own
            probabilities. If she always serves to the backhand, what's left to
            average over is what the receiver does, so use <m>.7</m> and
            <m>.3</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.7(.4) + .3(.7) = .49</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            You read down the <m>b</m> column. The server's strategies are the
            rows, so <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2)</m> lives in the <m>b</m> row.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    The same computation on the <m>f</m> row gives
    <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = .7(.7) + .3(.1) = .52</m>, which is the same
    number!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-tennis-any-p">
    <statement>
      <p>
        We know <m>\pi_1(b, \sigma_2) = .52</m> and
        <m>\pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = .52</m>. For any <m>p</m>, what is
        <m>\pi_1(pb + (1-p)f, \sigma_2)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>.52p</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That only counts the serves to the backhand. The other
            <m>1-p</m> of the time she serves to the forehand, which also
            earns <m>.52</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>1.04</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            She can't collect both. Each serve goes one way or the other, so
            her payoff is a weighted average, not a sum.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p><m>.52</m> when <m>p = .6</m>, and less for other values of <m>p</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Tempting, but try <m>p = 0</m> or <m>p = 1</m>. She still gets
            <m>.52</m>. Against <m>q = .7</m>, her choice of <m>p</m> doesn't
            change her payoff at all.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>.52</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. By (5.6) in the book, it's
            <m>p(.52) + (1-p)(.52) = .52</m>. Averaging two equal numbers
            gives that number back, no matter the weights.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So there is no way for the server to improve her payoff when the
    receiver plays <m>\sigma_2</m>. The receiver's side works the same way.
    Against <m>p = .6</m>, both of her pure strategies earn her <m>.48</m>, so
    no mix does better for her either. That means
    <m>(.6b + .4f,\ .7b + .3f)</m> is a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-1-theorems">
    <title>Section 5.1, Nash's Theorem and the Fundamental Theorem</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read from Theorem 5.1 through the paragraph right after Theorem
      5.2, the one beginning <q>This theorem just says.</q></alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      You can skip the proof of Theorem 5.2 (but read it if you're
      interested!). Before moving on, we'll apply Theorem 5.2 to the Tennis
      game. Then we'll use Tennis to reason out the next few theorems before
      reading them.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Notice that condition (1) of Theorem 5.2 is exactly what we just found
    in Tennis 🤯. The server's active strategies both earn <m>.52</m> (with
    the receiver fixed at her equilibrium strategy), and similarly the
    receiver's both earn <m>.48</m>. What about condition (2)?
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-condition-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Do we need to check condition (2) of Theorem 5.2 for the Tennis
        equilibrium <m>(.6b + .4f,\ .7b + .3f)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. Condition (2) is about inactive strategies, and both players
            use both strategies, so there aren't any.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Condition (2) only matters when someone leaves a strategy
            unused. In Tennis, condition (1) is all there is to check.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            No, because condition (1) already holds.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Right answer, wrong reason. Condition (1) says nothing about
            inactive strategies. The real reason is that there aren't any
            here. If the server never served to the backhand, we'd have to
            check condition (2) too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. Every Nash equilibrium has to satisfy both conditions.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Both conditions do have to hold, but condition (2) only says
            something when a player has an inactive strategy. Here both
            players use both strategies, so there's nothing to check.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's reason out the next three theorems before reading them.
  </p>

  <example xml:id="eg-tennis-equilibrium-payoff">
    <title>The server's payoff at the Tennis equilibrium</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Earlier we found that against the receiver's equilibrium strategy
        <m>\sigma_2 = .7b + .3f</m>, both of the server's pure strategies
        earn the same amount:
        <me>
          \pi_1(b, \sigma_2) = .52 \quad\text{and}\quad \pi_1(f, \sigma_2) = .52
        </me>
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            What is the server's payoff at the equilibrium, where she plays
            <m>.6b + .4f</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            What is her payoff if she switches to any other mix
            <m>pb + (1-p)f</m>, with the receiver still at <m>\sigma_2</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Both come straight from (5.6), which says her payoff is a weighted
        average of what <m>b</m> and <m>f</m> earn:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\pi_1(.6b + .4f,\ \sigma_2) = .6(.52) + .4(.52) = .52</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\pi_1(pb + (1-p)f,\ \sigma_2) = p(.52) + (1-p)(.52) = .52</m>,
            no matter what <m>p</m> is. (This is the same thing you found in
            the <m>.52</m> checkpoint!)
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    That's <alert>Theorem 5.3</alert>. At a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,
    all of a player's active strategies earn the same amount, call it
    <m>K</m> (for the server in Tennis, <m>K = .52</m>). Then:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Her equilibrium payoff is <m>K</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Any mix of her active strategies also earns <m>K</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    Next, pure strategies are special mixed strategies. So, we can consider
    whether a strategy profile is a Nash equilibrium both in the sense of
    Chapter 3 and in the sense of this chapter, where we also have mixed
    strategies. Let's connect them with the checkpoint below!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-pure-vs-mixed-deviation">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(s_1^*, s_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium in the Chapter 3
        sense, so Player 1 can't improve her payoff by switching to any pure
        strategy. Could she improve by switching to some mixed strategy
        <m>\tau_1</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. A mix can combine the strengths of several pure strategies.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Against a fixed opponent, a mix is just a weighted average of its
            pure strategies' payoffs. An average can never beat the largest
            thing you're averaging.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            You'd have to check every possible mix to know.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's exactly the work (5.6) saves you. Every mix is a weighted
            average of pure strategy payoffs, so checking the pure strategies
            is enough.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. By (5.6), <m>\tau_1</m>'s payoff is a weighted average of her
            pure strategies' payoffs, and none of those beat her payoff at
            the equilibrium.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. If every number you're averaging is at most
            <m>\pi_1(s^*)</m>, the average is too.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    So a Nash equilibrium in the Chapter 3 sense is also a mixed strategy
    Nash equilibrium. The converse is even more clear: if no player can
    improve by switching to any mixed strategy, then in particular no player
    can improve by switching to a pure one. Together, these give
    <alert>Theorem 5.4</alert>: for pure strategy profiles, the new
    definition of Nash equilibrium agrees with the old one.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-tennis-not-strict">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Recall that a Nash equilibrium is <term>strict</term> if every player
        does strictly worse by switching. Is the Tennis equilibrium strict?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes, since it's the only Nash equilibrium of the game.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Being the only equilibrium and being strict are different things.
            Strictness is about whether switching can ever tie.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            No. The server can switch to always serving to the backhand and
            still get <m>.52</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. By Theorem 5.3, any mix of her active strategies earns
            the same, so she has plenty of switches that tie.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Yes. No player can do better by switching.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's just the definition of a Nash equilibrium. Strict means
            every switch makes you <em>worse</em>, and here some switches
            leave the server exactly where she was.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    That's <alert>Theorem 5.5</alert>. Only pure equilibria can be strict.
    Whenever a player mixes (uses more than one active strategy) at a Nash
    equilibrium, Theorem 5.3 says she can switch to another active strategy
    and tie, so the equilibrium can't be strict.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-1-rest">
    <title>Section 5.1, The Rest</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read the rest of Section 5.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      We just reasoned through Theorems 5.3, 5.4, and 5.5, so this should go
      quickly. The last two paragraphs add some discussion beyond these
      theorems.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>
